  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe area remains locked in a broad southwest flow pattern between \nthe offshore ridge and a trough over the Midwest to Great Lakes \nregion. The upper level trough will slowly move east heading into \nthe weekend, pushing the ridge further offshore. A west-southwest \nflow of deep moisture will persist over the area, with PWATs around \n1.9 inches today, 1.8 inches Friday and falling slightly to 1.6 \ninches on Saturday. Surface dew points will remain elevated in the \nupper 60s to low 70s, locally mid 70s, through Saturday as well. Each \nafternoon and evening instability will build due to the day time \nheating. MLCAPE is progged to be 1000-1500 J/kg this afternoon, and \nagain on Friday and Saturday afternoon as well. Shear will be 20-30 \nkts each afternoon and evening, which may be enough for a few \nclusters of thunderstorms to develop. There will be the potential \nfor isolated gusty to damaging winds and torrential downpours with \nthese storms. There will be a little more forcing heading into the \narea for Friday and Saturday as the broad trough approaches, this \nmay also help to better organize the convection at times.\n\nOtherwise, outside of the showers and thunderstorms expect \nconditions to be very warm and humid under partly cloudy skies. \nDaytime highs will be generally in the 80s, with a few areas around \n90 in the urban valley locations. Heat indices could reach the low \nto mid-90s this afternoon for locations that see more sunshine and \nmiss out on the showers/storms. Did not have enough confidence or \ncoverage of mid 90s heat indices to issue any heat advisories at \nthis time. The heat index should be slightly lower Friday and \nSaturday afternoons, mainly in the upper 80s to low 90s, except mid \n90s in the Wyoming Valley. Overnight lows will be mild and muggy, in \nthe mid to upper 60s with areas of fog possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMost of the latest deterministic and ensemble guidance swings a \nfront through and east of the area later Saturday night, bringing a \ndrier air mass in behind it. The 12z NAM seems to be an outlier, \nwith a slower frontal passage on Sunday. The official forecast \nfollows the majority of the ensembles which brings sunny skies, \nfalling dew points and dry weather areawide. Temperatures remain \nseasonably warm in the upper 70s to mid-80s, with steady NW winds \nbetween 6-12 mph expected. It will be a little cooler Sunday night \nand less humid, with lows in the upper 50s to mid-60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfter the brief, flat ridge exits Sunday night, the next fast moving \nshortwave disturbance will approach from the west on Monday. \nMoisture levels will increase, with surface dew points rising back \ninto the upper 60s. Chances for showers and thunderstorms increase \nMonday afternoon and evening as this disturbance rolls through. \nTemperatures warm up a few degrees compared to Sunday. The overall \nflow then looks to remain fairly zonal Tuesday and Wednesday, before \na deeper trough digs into the Central Great Lakes midweek. This will \nkeep fast moving shortwave moving into our area at times. Tuesday \nhas the highest chance for showers and thunderstorms, with chances \nfading a bit by Wednesday if current timing and placement of weather \nsystems hold. Temperatures start a gradual cooling trend Tuesday, \nWednesday and Thursday next week; falling back to near average \nlevels for the middle of August. A deeper, more amplified upper \nlevel trough looks to approach the area for the end of next week; \nthis could act to increase showers and storms along with brining \neven cooler temperatures behind it for next weekend.",
